# Line endings are pinned to LF.
#
# Prod is Linux and the deploy rsyncs this working tree straight into
# /home/offda9/public_html. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true at SYSTEM
# level, and this repo pinned nothing, so a fresh checkout produced CRLF while
# prod holds LF. Verified 2026-08-11: force-checking-out customer/login.php made
# it diverge from prod's byte-identical copy. Left unpinned, the first rsync
# deploy would rewrite EVERY text file on prod as a CRLF diff-storm — and CRLF
# genuinely breaks shell scripts and can upset .htaccess directives.
#
# Same class the platform repo closed with `tools/git-hooks/* text eol=lf`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be line-ending converted or diffed as text.
*.pdf  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.zip  binary
*.sqlite binary

# Git hooks are EXTENSION-LESS, so a *.sh rule never reaches them. A CRLF
# checkout makes Linux/WSL reject the hook as `bad interpreter: env bash^M`;
# git then SKIPS it silently and every fix commit lands ungated.
tools/git-hooks/* text eol=lf
